+## Suffix splice with MTP or DFlash
+
+Single-module MTP, standard DFlash, and DFlash2 can combine model drafts with
+suffix continuations from the request history and the cross-request corpus. Every
+request still publishes one linear draft, so target verification and sampling
+semantics are unchanged.
+
+For example, Qwen3.8-27B-FP8 uses its matching DFlash2 checkpoint with seven
+speculative tokens (the checkpoint's block size is eight):
+
+```bash
+vllm serve Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B-FP8 \
+  --language-model-only \
+  --reasoning-parser qwen3 \
+  --speculative-config '{
+    "method": "dflash",
+    "model": "incoai/Qwen3.8-27B-DFlash2",
+    "num_speculative_tokens": 7,
+    "suffix_hybrid_mode": "splice",
+    "suffix_hybrid_prefix_length": 1,
+    "suffix_hybrid_min_match_length": 4
+  }'
+```
+
+The generic configuration is:
+
+```python
+speculative_config = {
+    "method": "dflash",
+    "model": "<matching-dflash-model>",
+    "num_speculative_tokens": 15,
+    "suffix_hybrid_mode": "splice",
+    "suffix_hybrid_prefix_length": 1,
+    "suffix_hybrid_min_match_length": 4,
+}
+```
+
+Splice runs the model drafter for the full batch, then uses the first model
+tokens as a temporary lookup prefix and may replace the remaining tail on the
+GPU. Lookups discard candidates that cannot fill all `K - prefix` tokens before
+ranking them. Splice keeps the model chain when its tail is identical to the
+historical tail, so acceptance feedback is attributed to the model chain.
+
+Acceptance statistics are indexed by source, match-length bucket, and absolute
+draft position. Model and suffix comparisons use the same candidate bucket when
+one is available. Model maturity is evaluated at the first replaced position.
+Structured-output rows keep their model drafts and do not update these
+statistics; they also skip suffix lookup. Other rows continue to provide
+feedback. Lookup scans are bounded by the current batch's history-length upper
+bound, not just `max_model_len`. Rows without newly sampled tokens, empty
+corpora, blocks without a full continuation, and locally maximal matches skip
+avoidable work. Finished-request corpus ingestion uses the scheduler's final
+trimmed token length, carried with the next completion notification. Requests
+using string `stop` and aborted requests are excluded from the shared corpus so
+front-end-trimmed tokens cannot become other requests' candidates; request-local
+matching is unaffected. Set
+`suffix_hybrid_min_expected_accepted` to add an expected acceptance
+threshold after the statistics mature. Counts are halved every
+`suffix_hybrid_stats_half_life` feedback steps (default 1024).
+`suffix_hybrid_exploration_interval` is disabled by default. A positive interval
+N inverts the mature routing decision every N steps per batch row, collecting
+fresh comparison samples for both routes. Exploration still requires a valid
+full-length candidate and can override the expected-acceptance threshold.
+`suffix_hybrid_corpus_key_mode` defaults to `dual`. The experimental `four` and
+`four_fallback` settings use an 8-slot 4-token specialization, optionally
+checking the 2-token table only when no valid 4-token candidate exists. They can
+change candidates and recall, so they are intended for ablation rather than a
+default behavior change.
+
+Splice requires greedy drafting and standard verification. It does not support
+adaptive verification, `prefill_context_kv_only`, watermarking, DBO, or
+microbatching. The prefix must be positive and smaller than
+`num_speculative_tokens`. The minimum match length must be positive and no
+greater than `suffix_decoding_max_tree_depth`. The expected acceptance threshold
+must be between zero and `num_speculative_tokens`, inclusive.
+
+The corpus limitations are the same as for [suffix decoding](suffix.md). Splice
+also uses process-wide acceptance statistics for routing, so seeded sampling is
+not guaranteed to be reproducible across unrelated traffic even when
+`suffix_decoding_corpus_tokens=0`; that setting disables token-corpus sharing,
+not routing-statistic sharing. Splice retains a dense GPU token history, so
+account for `max_num_seqs * max_model_len * 4` bytes in addition to the corpus
+and lookup scratch space.
+
